### How does work-stealing work?

Work-stealing is a load-balancing strategy where idle threads take work from busy threads. In ForkJoinPool, each worker has a double-ended queue. The owner pushes and pops from the front (LIFO). Thieves steal from the back (FIFO). This design keeps all cores busy with minimal synchronization overhead.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.RecursiveTask;
import java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SumTransactionsTask extends RecursiveTask<BigDecimal> {
    private final BigDecimal[] amounts;
    private final int lo, hi;
    private static final int THRESHOLD = 1000;

    SumTransactionsTask(BigDecimal[] amounts, int lo, int hi) {
        this.amounts = amounts;
        this.lo = lo;
        this.hi = hi;
    }

    @Override
    protected BigDecimal compute() {
        if (hi - lo <= THRESHOLD) {
            BigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;
            for (int i = lo; i < hi; i++) sum = sum.add(amounts[i]);
            return sum;
        }
        int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
        SumTransactionsTask left = new SumTransactionsTask(amounts, lo, mid);
        left.fork();  // push onto my deque
        SumTransactionsTask right = new SumTransactionsTask(amounts, mid, hi);
        BigDecimal rightResult = right.compute();  // compute right now
        BigDecimal leftResult = left.join();        // get left result
        return leftResult.add(rightResult);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] amounts = new BigDecimal[1_000_000];
        for (int i = 0; i < amounts.length; i++)
            amounts[i] = new BigDecimal("100.00");
        ForkJoinPool pool = new ForkJoinPool();
        BigDecimal total = pool.invoke(
                new SumTransactionsTask(amounts, 0, amounts.length));
        System.out.println("Total: " + total);
    }
}
```

### Why is blocking IO on the common pool dangerous?

The common pool is sized to approximately the number of CPU cores. If workers block on IO, they cannot process tasks or steal from other workers. Every parallel stream and default CompletableFuture in the entire application shares this pool.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class CommonPoolDangerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // DANGEROUS: blocking HTTP calls on the common pool
        IntStream.range(0, 100).parallel().forEach(i -> {
            // Each worker blocks for 200ms on HTTP call
            // All common pool workers stuck!
            // parallelStream in OTHER parts of the app: STALLED
            // CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(): STALLED
            httpCall("http://api.bank.com/balance/" + i);
        });
    }

    static String httpCall(String url) {
        try { Thread.sleep(200); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
        return "response";
    }
}
```

### How do you fix the common pool blocking problem?

Use a dedicated bounded ThreadPoolExecutor for IO-bound work. Keep the common pool reserved for CPU-bound computation.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DedicatedPoolDemo {
    private static final ExecutorService ioPool =
            Executors.newFixedThreadPool(50);  // dedicated IO pool

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            CompletableFuture.runAsync(
                    () -> httpCall("http://api.bank.com/balance/" + id),
                    ioPool);  // explicit executor, NOT common pool
        }
        // Common pool: free for CPU-bound parallelStream
    }

    static void httpCall(String url) {
        try { Thread.sleep(200); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
    }
}
```

### What is ManagedBlocker?

ForkJoinPool has a compensation mechanism: when a worker blocks through a ManagedBlocker interface, the pool may create a temporary worker to maintain parallelism. However, this is not a reliable solution for bulk IO because it can create many compensation threads.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Why LIFO for the owner? The most recently forked task shares data with the parent task, making it hot in the CPU cache. Processing it first exploits temporal locality.
- Why FIFO for the thief? The oldest task is higher in the recursion tree and represents a larger subtree with more work, which minimizes the number of steal operations needed.
